Subject: [PATCH] Kandelo optimization: launch simple dinit services via posix_spawn

PURPOSE (read this first)
=========================
This patch is a deliberate OPTIMIZATION FOR KANDELO, not a package-specific
hack or workaround. It changes nothing about dinit's semantics or its service
contract; it only chooses a cheaper kernel primitive for the common case.

Why it exists
-------------
On the Kandelo kernel, an ordinary fork() is expensive: the child pays an
eager memory copy plus a wasm-fork-instrument continuation replay before it
can reach exec. When dinit launches a service it does fork()+execvp(), so it
pays that whole cost and then throws the reconstructed child state away
microseconds later at exec. That is pure waste for the spawn-then-exec pattern.

musl's posix_spawn() issues CLONE_VM|CLONE_VFORK, which routes to Kandelo's
vfork path: the child borrows the parent's memory and execs immediately,
skipping the continuation replay entirely. This is exactly the right primitive
for "start a process and immediately exec it", which is what every service
launch is.

What it does
------------
Adds a posix_spawn fast path in base_process_service::start_ps_process for
"simple" service launches — no socket activation, no readiness-notification
fd, no control-socket fd, default stdin, no uid change, no rlimits, not on the
console, and a NONE/LOGFILE log with no ownership change. The child setup for
that case reduces exactly to what posix_spawn's file actions and attributes
express faithfully (stdin/out/err redirection, chdir, new session, restored
signal mask, environment). ANY service that needs more than that — and any
spawn error — falls through to the UNCHANGED fork()+run_child_proc path, which
also reports the precise failing exec stage. So behavior is identical; only
the cheap case gets cheaper.

Scope and honesty
-----------------
This speeds up dinit's own service launches (the fork-then-EXEC path). It is a
general platform micro-win: any fork-then-exec spawner benefits. It does NOT,
by itself, speed up workloads dominated by pure fork() with no exec — e.g.
php-fpm's static worker prefork, where vfork cannot apply because the child
keeps running as a worker. Measured on the WordPress SQLite browser demo, the
dinit service-launch window is a small fraction of boot; the dominant cost is
the services' own startup (php-fpm worker prefork + extension MINIT). This
patch is worth landing as a correct, low-risk optimization, not as a fix for
any single demo's boot time.

Correctness
-----------
The fork fallback is structurally unchanged. The posix_spawn path preserves
dinit's parent-side state machine: it reuses the same exec-status pipe (the
CLOEXEC write end closes on the child's exec, so the parent reads EOF ==
success), arms the same dasynq child-reap watch on the spawned pid, and falls
through to the existing parent bookkeeping. dinit's existing scripted-service
integration test exercises this fast path.

